(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-infective-endocarditis-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Infective Endocarditis Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 41-year-old man who injects drugs is being treated for tricuspid valve endocarditis. On day 2 he develops a new cough, and chest imaging shows several new scattered lesions in both lungs. Which complication best explains these findings?","choices":["Aspiration of vomit","Septic pulmonary emboli","Drug-induced lung injury","Left heart failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lung is among the listed embolic complications of infective endocarditis, and septic pulmonary infarcts count as a vascular phenomenon under the modified Duke criteria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with acute endocarditis asks why the team is so worried when she felt well a week ago. Which statement about untreated acute endocarditis is accurate?","choices":["It can be fatal within weeks","It resolves without treatment","It smolders harmlessly for years","It is mainly a lung infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute endocarditis rapidly damages cardiac structures and seeds extracardiac sites; if untreated, it progresses to death within weeks. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F). Two separate blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus, a transesophageal echocardiogram shows no vegetation, and he has no predisposing heart condition, no drug use and no skin, eye or kidney findings. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Possible endocarditis","Definite endocarditis","Endocarditis ruled out","No Duke criteria met"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. He has one major criterion (a typical organism, Staphylococcus aureus, in two separate cultures) and one minor criterion (fever), which makes endocarditis possible. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman with previous infective endocarditis brings an old wallet card that lists clindamycin for her dental prophylaxis. She has a penicillin allergy. Which counseling point is most accurate?","choices":["Clindamycin is dropped","Keep using clindamycin","Switch to amoxicillin","She no longer needs any"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is no longer recommended for prophylaxis because of the risk of Clostridioides difficile infection; she should be given an alternative such as azithromycin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with native mitral valve endocarditis after dental work has blood cultures growing penicillin-susceptible viridans streptococci. Which antibiotic is a directed choice?","choices":["Daptomycin alone","Penicillin G or ceftriaxone","Nafcillin or oxacillin","Rifampin plus gentamicin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococci are treated with penicillin G or ceftriaxone, with or without gentamicin, or vancomycin if penicillin cannot be used. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 9-year-old with unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease is scheduled for a tonsillectomy. Which is the most appropriate step regarding endocarditis?","choices":["Give antibiotics afterward instead","Cancel the tonsillectomy","Give prophylaxis before surgery","No prophylaxis is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Unrepaired cyanotic congenital heart disease is a high-risk pediatric condition, and tonsillectomy is an invasive respiratory procedure with mucosal incision, so prophylaxis is given beforehand. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with a prosthetic mitral valve has a fever of 38.7 °C (101.7 °F) and Osler nodes (painful red nodules on the pads of her fingers). Under the modified Duke criteria, which category do the Osler nodes fall into?","choices":["Predisposition","Major blood culture criterion","Immunologic phenomenon","Vascular phenomenon"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Osler nodes are painful and immunologic; with glomerulonephritis, Roth spots and rheumatoid factor they make up the immunologic minor criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has subacute endocarditis, and her blood cultures grow Eikenella corrodens, a member of the HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) group. Which antibiotic is most appropriate?","choices":["Rifampin","Ceftriaxone","Nafcillin","Vancomycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. HACEK (Haemophilus, Aggregatibacter, Cardiobacterium, Eikenella, Kingella) endocarditis is treated with ceftriaxone, or ampicillin-sulbactam. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man who lives at home has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and a new murmur. He has had no recent hospital stay, procedure or urinary symptoms, and no primary source of infection is found. Two separate blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis. How do these cultures count under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Not counted until echocardiography","A major criterion","A minor criterion","Excluded as contaminants"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Community-acquired enterococci in the absence of a primary focus count as a typical organism, so growth from two separate blood cultures meets the major blood culture criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with native aortic valve endocarditis and a large vegetation asks whether a blood thinner would prevent a stroke. He has no other reason for anticoagulation. Which choice should be avoided?","choices":["Starting heparin or warfarin","Continuing his antibiotics","Monitoring renal function","Consulting infectious disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation is not beneficial in patients with native valve infection (it is controversial with prosthetic valves), so it should not be started for this reason. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with fever has four sets of blood cultures drawn. One set grows coagulase-negative staphylococci, and the other three are negative. How should this single positive culture be counted under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Excluded as a likely contaminant","As the major blood culture criterion","As a vascular phenomenon","As definite proof of endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Single positive cultures for coagulase-negative staphylococci (and diphtheroids) are excluded from the microbiologic criterion because they are common culture contaminants. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with a prosthetic aortic valve is scheduled for a screening colonoscopy and has no active infection. She asks whether she needs antibiotics beforehand. Which advice is most accurate?","choices":["Amoxicillin 30-60 minutes before","A 7-day antibiotic course","Intravenous vancomycin before","No prophylaxis is needed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gastrointestinal and genitourinary procedures such as colonoscopy, upper endoscopy and cystoscopy do not require endocarditis prophylaxis, even in high-risk patients, absent active infection. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"An 83-year-old woman is ready for discharge on day 9 of a planned 6-week course of intravenous antibiotics for endocarditis. She is deconditioned and cannot yet walk safely on her own. Which part of the multidisciplinary plan addresses this?","choices":["Immediate valve surgery","Stopping the antibiotics","Rehabilitation placement","Addiction medicine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The multidisciplinary team plans disposition, including rehabilitation, while infectious disease continues antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man who injects drugs has a fever of 38.8 °C (101.8 °F) and Roth spots (white-centered retinal hemorrhages) on fundoscopy, meeting three minor Duke criteria. He has received no antibiotics. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure him that it is not endocarditis","Order a chest radiograph and discharge","Draw blood cultures from separate sites","Start oral amoxicillin and recheck later"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Blood cultures (three sets from separate sites) come before any antibiotic; a positive culture with a typical organism would add a major criterion and make the case definite. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has 3 weeks of low-grade fever and malaise that began a week after a dental extraction. A new murmur of mitral regurgitation is heard. Three sets of blood cultures are drawn. Which organism are they most likely to grow?","choices":["Enterococci","Candida species","Viridans streptococci","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The oral cavity is the source of viridans streptococci, the classic cause of subacute endocarditis after dental procedures and the organism dental prophylaxis targets. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man will finish 5 more weeks of intravenous antibiotics for endocarditis after discharge. Which consultant manages his antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring?","choices":["Addiction medicine","Dermatology","Infectious disease","Cardiothoracic surgery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Infectious disease manages antibiotic therapy and outpatient monitoring pending disposition, as part of the multidisciplinary team. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who injects drugs has 5 days of high fever and rigors. Transthoracic imaging shows a large vegetation on the tricuspid valve, and three sets of blood cultures are drawn from separate sites. Which organism are the cultures most likely to grow?","choices":["Viridans streptococci","Enterococcus faecalis","Candida albicans","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In people who inject drugs, Staphylococcus aureus causes more than 60% of all endocarditis and 80-90% of cases where the tricuspid valve is infected. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with a malar rash, painful joints and a positive antinuclear antibody test has a new murmur. She has no fever, repeated blood cultures are negative, and echocardiography shows small sterile-appearing valve vegetations. Which is the most likely cause of her valve lesion?","choices":["Subacute infective endocarditis","Acute infective endocarditis","Device endocarditis","Libman-Sacks endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Systemic lupus erythematosus may present with non-infective (Libman-Sacks) endocarditis, which mimics infective endocarditis in the autoimmune differential. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man with a prosthetic mitral valve is booked for a diagnostic bronchoscopy. No biopsy or mucosal incision is planned. Which advice about endocarditis prophylaxis is most accurate?","choices":["Required before every bronchoscopy","A week of oral amoxicillin","Intravenous vancomycin first","None is needed for this procedure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bronchoscopy does not require prophylaxis unless it involves incision of the respiratory mucosa, such as a biopsy. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"An 85-year-old man with degenerative aortic valve disease presents with fever and a new murmur. His family is surprised that a heart infection could occur at his age. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Age lowers the risk","Over-60s are over half of cases","It occurs mainly in drug users","It occurs mainly in children"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Risk is increased among the elderly: patients over 60 account for more than 50% of cases, and degenerative valve disease is now a leading predisposing condition. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with subacute endocarditis has a fundoscopic examination that shows Roth spots (white-centered retinal hemorrhages). What process causes these spots?","choices":["Optic nerve swelling from pressure","Hypertensive retinal changes","Vasculitis of small retinal arteries","Retinal detachment from trauma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Roth spots are white-centered hemorrhages from vasculitis affecting the small arteries of the retina, grouped with the immunologic phenomena in the Duke criteria. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a fever, a new murmur and Janeway lesions (painless macules on her soles), and two blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus. Her transesophageal echocardiogram is read as normal. Which conclusion is correct?","choices":["She has a skin infection instead","Endocarditis is not ruled out","Endocarditis is now excluded","The cultures were contaminated"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A normal transesophageal echocardiogram does not necessarily rule out endocarditis; Staphylococcus aureus in two separate cultures still meets a major criterion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has been in the hospital for 9 days after hip surgery. She develops a new fever, and two sets of blood cultures grow Staphylococcus aureus; she has no indwelling catheter, and examination finds no wound or other source. Which diagnosis must be suspected?","choices":["Contamination of both culture sets","Postoperative atelectasis","Drug fever from antibiotics","Health care-associated endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Health care-associated endocarditis should be suspected in hospitalized patients who develop fevers and positive blood cultures without an apparent source; Staphylococcus aureus causes about half of these cases. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 7-year-old with congenital heart disease is admitted with viridans streptococcal endocarditis. Which approach to antibiotic therapy is most appropriate?","choices":["Intramuscular, fixed adult dose","Oral, for one week","Observation without antibiotics","Intravenous, weight-based, prolonged"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pediatric therapy is essentially the same as adult: prolonged, bactericidal, parenteral (intravenous, not intramuscular), pathogen-directed and weight-based. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman is brought in by her daughter for 2 days of new confusion. She has a fever of 38.9 °C (102.0 °F), a new murmur and conjunctival petechiae (pinpoint hemorrhages on the conjunctiva). Brain imaging shows multiple small scattered lesions without a large stroke. Which mechanism best explains her confusion?","choices":["Low-flow ischemia from heart block","A side effect of sedation","Septic cerebral emboli","A primary bacterial meningitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vegetations shower septic emboli into the systemic circulation; the brain is affected (ischemic stroke, abscess, hemorrhage, meningitis), producing neurologic deficits such as confusion. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 4-month-old with tachycardia, poor feeding, respiratory distress and hypotension is being worked up for sepsis. A new murmur is heard. Why must endocarditis be considered even though the presentation looks like sepsis?","choices":["Infants show classic Osler nodes","Infants have marked joint pain","Infants rarely develop sepsis","Infants present nonspecifically"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In neonates and infants endocarditis presents nonspecifically and is often indistinguishable from sepsis or heart failure: feeding difficulty, respiratory distress, tachycardia, hypotension and a new or changing murmur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has 6 weeks of aching and stiffness in her shoulders and hips, fatigue and a low-grade fever. Her erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein are high, but no murmur is heard and three sets of blood cultures are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Polymyalgia rheumatica","Subacute endocarditis","Atrial myxoma","Antiphospholipid syndrome"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Polymyalgia rheumatica sits in the differential of endocarditis because it causes myalgia with elevated inflammatory markers, but there is no murmur and cultures are negative. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with a prosthetic mitral valve is having a gum biopsy. She is not allergic to penicillin, but she cannot swallow pills after a recent stroke. Which prophylaxis is most appropriate?","choices":["No prophylaxis","Ampicillin by injection","Oral amoxicillin","Injected clindamycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A patient without penicillin allergy who cannot take oral medication receives ampicillin intramuscularly or intravenously (or cefazolin or ceftriaxone) 30-60 minutes before. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has enterococcal endocarditis of the aortic valve after recurrent urinary infections. Which outcome is he at notable risk for?","choices":["Septic lung emboli","Needing heart surgery","Spontaneous cure","Lowest death rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Enterococcal endocarditis is difficult to treat because of antibiotic resistance, and major complications with need for valve replacement occur in about 30-40%. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man on chemotherapy has Candida endocarditis confirmed on blood cultures and echocardiography. Which referral is most appropriate in addition to infectious disease?","choices":["Cardiothoracic surgery","Addiction medicine","Physical therapy","Dermatology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fungal infection is difficult to cure with medications alone and is an indication for surgery, so cardiothoracic surgery evaluates him. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 33"}]};
